course: dictionary
lesson: 2779
title: Disgusting and its many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «disgusting» tells you something is very unpleasant, so unpleasant that it makes you feel sick or strongly dislike it. Look at the words around it to be sure. «The bin smelled disgusting in the summer heat.» The words bin, smelled and summer heat are clues: a hot bin full of rubbish has a horrible smell.
explain: «Disgusting» can also describe behaviour that is very bad or unfair. Swap your meaning into the sentence to check it. «Taking the other team's ball was disgusting» makes sense if you read it as very unfair.
explain: Be careful with manners. Calling someone's food «disgusting» is rude, even if you mean it. Say «I don't like it, thank you» instead.
explain: Other places have their own words for it. In Britain, «minging» means disgusting, and in Ireland, «Rank!» is slang for the same thing.
fact: Disgusting is an adjective: disgusting, more disgusting, most disgusting.
fact: «Disgusting» means very unpleasant, making you feel sick or strongly dislike something.
fact: «Disgusting» can also describe very bad or unfair behaviour.
fact: Useful phrases are «absolutely disgusting» and «smells disgusting».
fact: Regional words: «minging» (Britain) means disgusting; «Rank!» (Ireland) is slang for disgusting.
exercise: «The bin smelled disgusting in the summer heat.» What does disgusting mean here? | Quite funny | Very shiny and clean | Too quiet to hear | *Very unpleasant, making you feel sick
exercise: Which is the polite way to say you don't like a meal? | *I don't like it, thank you | Your food is disgusting | Yuck, that's rude | Give me another one
exercise: «He told a cruel lie about his friend. That was disgusting.» What does disgusting describe? | A colour | A smell | *Very bad, unfair behaviour | A dog's walk
test: Which word is the strongest form of disgusting? | *most disgusting | disgust | disgusting | more disgusting
test: In Britain, which word means disgusting? | eejit | tamping | greetin' | *minging
exam: Which sentence uses disgusting to describe unfair behaviour rather than a smell? | The drains were disgusting | *Stealing the other team's ball was disgusting | The bin was disgusting | The soup smelled disgusting
